# E. coli NGS Data Analysis Workflow

This repository contains a Jupyter notebook (`Ecoli_Analysis.ipynb`) demonstrating a complete Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS) analysis pipeline — from raw read download through quality control, alignment, and variant calling — using publicly available *Escherichia coli* sequencing data from NCBI's Sequence Read Archive (SRA).

## Overview

The notebook walks through a standard bacterial resequencing workflow:

1. Download raw sequencing reads from SRA
2. Quality control and adapter/quality trimming
3. Alignment to a reference genome
4. Post-alignment processing (SAM → sorted, indexed BAM)
5. Variant calling (SNPs and Indels) and summary statistics

**Sample used:** `SRR2584863` (single-end reads)
**Reference genome:** *Escherichia coli* B str. REL606 (NCBI accession `GCA_000017985.1_ASM1798v1`)

## Tools Used

| Tool | Purpose |
|---|---|
| [SRA Toolkit](https://github.com/ncbi/sra-tools) (`prefetch`, `fastq-dump`) | Download and convert SRA data to FASTQ |
| [FastQC](https://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/projects/fastqc/) | Raw and post-trimming read quality assessment |
| [fastp](https://github.com/OpenGene/fastp) | Read trimming, quality filtering, adapter removal |
| [BWA](https://github.com/lh3/bwa) (`bwa mem`) | Alignment of reads to the reference genome |
| [Samtools](https://www.htslib.org/) | SAM→BAM conversion, sorting, indexing, alignment stats |
| [BCFtools](https://samtools.github.io/bcftools/) | Variant calling (`mpileup`/`call`) and variant statistics |

All tools are installed via `apt-get`/`apt` within the notebook itself.

## Workflow Steps

### 1. Install Required Tools
Installs `fastqc` and `sra-toolkit` via `apt-get`.

### 2. Obtain and Prepare Sequencing Data
- `prefetch SRR2584863` — downloads the SRA accession
- `fastq-dump SRR2584863` — converts it to `SRR2584863.fastq`

### 3. Quality Control and Trimming
- `fastqc` run on the raw FASTQ file
- `fastp` used to trim adapters/low-quality bases → `SRR2584863_trimmed.fastq` (+ HTML report)
- `fastqc` re-run on the trimmed FASTQ to confirm quality improvement

### 4. Read Alignment to a Reference Genome
- Installs `bwa` and `samtools`
- Downloads the *E. coli* reference genome (`GCA_000017985.1_ASM1798v1_genomic.fna.gz`) from NCBI
- Indexes the reference with `bwa index`
- Aligns trimmed reads with `bwa mem` → `SRR2584863.sam`

### 5. Post-Alignment Processing
- Converts SAM → BAM (`samtools view -bS`)
- Sorts the BAM file (`samtools sort`)
- Indexes the sorted BAM (`samtools index`)
- Generates alignment summary statistics (`samtools flagstat`)

### 6. Variant Calling and Analysis
- Installs `bcftools`
- Calls variants with `bcftools mpileup | bcftools call -mv` → `variants.vcf`
- Inspects and counts variants
- Generates summary statistics with `bcftools stats` → `variant_stats.txt`

## Known Issues in the Notebook

A few cells in the notebook are exploratory/erroneous and are **not part of the working pipeline**:
- **`cutadapt`** installation and execution cells fail (`ModuleNotFoundError`) — `fastp` already handles trimming, so this step can be skipped.
- One `bwa mem` cell references a human reference genome (`GRCh38.primary_assembly.genome.fa`) and a different sample (`SRR2079547`) — this is a leftover from an unrelated analysis and does not apply to this *E. coli* workflow.
- One `bcftools mpileup` cell is a malformed re-run (line-continuation/path issue) — the correctly working version is the one immediately preceding it.

## Requirements

- Unix/Linux environment with `sudo`/`apt-get` access (e.g., Ubuntu)
- Jupyter Notebook or JupyterLab
- Internet access (to reach NCBI SRA and genome FTP servers)
- Recommended: at least 2 CPU cores (checked via `nproc` for `bwa mem -t`)

## Output Files

| File | Description |
|---|---|
| `SRR2584863.fastq` | Raw sequencing reads |
| `SRR2584863_trimmed.fastq` | Quality-trimmed reads |
| `SRR2584863_trimmed.html` | fastp trimming report |
| `GCA_000017985.1_ASM1798v1_genomic.fna.gz` | Reference genome (compressed) |
| `SRR2584863.sam` / `.bam` / `.sorted.bam` / `.sorted.bam.bai` | Alignment files |
| `variants.vcf` | Called variants (SNPs/Indels) |
| `variant_stats.txt` | Variant summary statistics |

## Usage

Open `Ecoli_Analysis.ipynb` in Jupyter and run the cells sequentially from top to bottom, skipping the known erroneous cells noted above (cutadapt cells and the human-genome `bwa mem` cell).

```bash
jupyter notebook Ecoli_Analysis.ipynb
```

